  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/assets/3a919c1ad631af6dfea304db2bdf0ad36fcbff6162f516e8591d7ce9cef3ecca.png)\n\nWhat are the cylindrical polar coordinates of the point $P?$",
  "worked_solution": "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where $r\\ge 0$ and $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/assets/c06f6a453581797596203059e6a1bfd30afd959f469f0408889c6c947a368809.png)\n\n \n\n- The ray from the origin through ${P}^{\u2032}$ makes an angle of $\\theta =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4}=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The distance from the origin to ${P}^{\u2032}$ is $r=1.$\n\n- The $z$-coordinate is $z=1$ because $P$ lies $1$ unit *below* the $xy$-plane.\n\nSo, the cylindrical coordinates of $P$ are $(1,\\frac{7\\pi }{4},-1).$",
